From: omarchy-dragon-sl7 <noreply@users.noreply.github.com>
Date: Mon, 5 Oct 2026 22:02:00 -0500
Subject: [PATCH] SL7 local: arm64: dts: qcom: x1e80100-romulus: Enable the Iris video codec

SL7 local: arm64: dts: qcom: x1e80100-romulus: Enable the Iris video codec

hamoa.dtsi describes the Iris video codec (video-codec@aa00000,
compatible "qcom,x1e80100-iris", "qcom,sm8550-iris") and leaves it
disabled because its firmware is signed by the OEM. Enable it for the
Surface Laptop 7 with the Microsoft signed image qcvss8380.mbn from the
Surface driver package (SurfaceUpdate/qcdx8380). Other Hamoa boards
enable the node the same way with their own vendor path (for example
qcom/x1e80100/LENOVO/21N1/qcvss8380.mbn for the ThinkPad T14s).

The node is in the shared romulus DTSI, so it covers both the 13.8 inch
(romulus13) and the 15 inch (romulus15). hamoa.dtsi already carries the
video_mem reserved region, the videocc controller and the clocks, power
domains, interconnects and IOMMU streams the driver needs, so only the
firmware name and the status are board specific.

The firmware is not part of any package here; omarchy-surface-sl7-firmware
installs it from the Microsoft MSI into
qcom/x1e80100/microsoft/Romulus/. Without the file the driver probe fails
with -ENOENT on firmware load and the node is otherwise inert.

Origin: ours
Upstream-Status: Not submitted (needs signed firmware in linux-firmware)
---
--- a/arch/arm64/boot/dts/qcom/x1e80100-microsoft-romulus.dtsi
+++ b/arch/arm64/boot/dts/qcom/x1e80100-microsoft-romulus.dtsi
@@ -1141,6 +1141,16 @@
 	};
 };
 
+&iris {
+	/*
+	 * The Iris video firmware is signed by the OEM; this is the Microsoft
+	 * signed image from the Surface driver package (qcdx8380).
+	 */
+	firmware-name = "qcom/x1e80100/microsoft/Romulus/qcvss8380.mbn";
+
+	status = "okay";
+};
+
 &lpass_tlmm {
 	spkr_01_sd_n_active: spkr-01-sd-n-active-state {
 		pins = "gpio12";
